        <p>
          Peristaltic and diaphragm pumps overlap in the low-to-mid flow range — both can dose chemicals, transfer fluids and run continuously — which is why buyers compare them so often. But they are built on completely different principles, and the right choice depends less on the pump's name and more on your fluid, your pressure, and how much you value uptime. Choose peristaltic for the wrong reason (or diaphragm for the wrong reason) and you will pay for it in consumables or in maintenance labor.
        </p>
        <p>
          This guide compares the two technologies across pumping principle, maintenance, flow accuracy, chemical resistance, pressure range and total cost of ownership — then gives you a checklist to make the call for your application.
        </p>

        <h2 id="how-peristaltic">How a Peristaltic Pump Works</h2>
        <p>
          A peristaltic pump moves fluid by squeezing a flexible tube. Rotating rollers press the tube against a fixed track, sealing a pocket of fluid and pushing it forward as the roller travels; when the roller passes, the tube springs back and draws in the next charge. The tube is the entire pump chamber — there are no valves, seals, impellers or internal parts to clog or leak. Flow is set by tube internal diameter, roller speed and the occlusion of the tube, and typical pump heads deliver repeatable flow in the region of ±1%, with precision heads bettering that.
        </p>
        <p>
          The practical consequences are distinctive: the fluid touches only the tube interior, the pump self-primes and tolerates brief dry running, slurries and viscous fluids pass through without clogging, and the pump is reversible simply by reversing the motor. The trade-off is that the tube is a consumable, and the maximum pressure is limited by what the tube can withstand — typically about 1–3 bar for laboratory heads and up to roughly 7–8 bar for reinforced industrial tubing.
        </p>

        <h2 id="how-diaphragm">How a Diaphragm Pump Works</h2>
        <p>
          A diaphragm pump displaces fluid with a flexible diaphragm that moves up and down (or back and forth) inside a chamber. On the down stroke the diaphragm pulls back, drawing fluid in through an inlet valve; on the up stroke it pushes fluid out through an outlet valve. The diaphragm is driven mechanically (by a connecting rod, often on an air-operated or motor-driven design) or hydraulically in precision metering pumps, where oil behind the diaphragm provides smoother, more accurate motion.
        </p>
        <p>
          Because the diaphragm can be made of robust engineered materials and the chamber is rigid, diaphragm pumps reach far higher pressures than peristaltic pumps — transfer types commonly handle 5–10 bar, and hydraulically actuated metering pumps can exceed 100 bar. The costs are the valve train and the diaphragm itself: check valves can be blocked by solids, the diaphragm is a wear item that needs periodic replacement, and dry running is generally not recommended because it can damage the diaphragm.
        </p>

